WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.568 --> 00:00:03.303
-Our first guest tonight is an
Emmy-winning writer and comedian

00:00:03.303 --> 00:00:06.706
you know from his work on "SNL,"
"Oh, Hello" on Broadway,

00:00:06.706 --> 00:00:08.407
and the Netflix series
"Big Mouth."

00:00:08.407 --> 00:00:10.075
He'll be playing
Madison Square Garden

00:00:10.075 --> 00:00:12.511
on June 23rd, 24th, and 25th as

00:00:12.511 --> 00:00:15.115
part of his "From Scratch"
stand-up tour.

00:00:15.115 --> 00:00:17.516
There it is right there,
"From Scratch."

00:00:17.516 --> 00:00:19.118
Please welcome back to the show,
our very good friend,

00:00:19.118 --> 00:00:20.953
John Mulaney, everybody!

00:00:20.953 --> 00:00:23.656
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:23.656 --> 00:00:31.998 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:31.998 --> 00:00:40.373 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:40.373 --> 00:00:41.674
Please.

00:00:41.674 --> 00:00:44.911 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:44.911 --> 00:00:46.178
Hello, old friend.

00:00:46.178 --> 00:00:48.114
-Hi, Seth, how are you?
-I'm good.

00:00:48.114 --> 00:00:50.550
I can't help but notice
that you brought a second suit.

00:00:50.550 --> 00:00:53.586
-There's no better way to bring
it out here than to hold it.

00:00:53.586 --> 00:00:55.021
[ Laughter ]

00:00:55.021 --> 00:00:58.324
You remember 18 months ago,
when you and all those people

00:00:58.324 --> 00:00:59.892
tricked me and confronted me

00:00:59.892 --> 00:01:02.094
and told me my life
was going downhill

00:01:02.094 --> 00:01:03.496
and I needed to
make some changes?

00:01:03.496 --> 00:01:04.597
-Yeah, yeah, yeah.
Uh-huh.

00:01:04.597 --> 00:01:06.098
-Let's talk about
the way you dress on air.

00:01:06.098 --> 00:01:09.101
-Oh, okay.
[ Laughter ]

00:01:09.101 --> 00:01:10.770
-They cured the pandemic.

00:01:10.770 --> 00:01:11.838
I mean, I don't read the news,

00:01:11.838 --> 00:01:13.773
but they cured the whole thing.
-Uh-huh.

00:01:13.773 --> 00:01:15.608
-We're not working
from home anymore.

00:01:15.608 --> 00:01:17.777
-No, no, no.
-You're not in an attic.

00:01:17.777 --> 00:01:19.912
-Yep.
-In Albany

00:01:19.912 --> 00:01:22.114
or wherever you go
when you're not here.

00:01:22.114 --> 00:01:24.350
-Yeah.
-You're still dressing

00:01:24.350 --> 00:01:26.986
like you had a date
and didn't want to wear

00:01:26.986 --> 00:01:28.988
the same clothes at work,
so you stopped at the Gap

00:01:28.988 --> 00:01:30.657
in Astor Place
and bought a sweater.

00:01:30.657 --> 00:01:31.558
[ Laughter ]

00:01:31.558 --> 00:01:33.393
It's -- I don't like to do this.

00:01:33.393 --> 00:01:34.660
You know I don't like to
confront people.

00:01:34.660 --> 00:01:36.529
-No.
-But how about a suit again?

00:01:36.529 --> 00:01:38.831
-I mean, if you want me to put
that on, I'll put that on.

00:01:38.831 --> 00:01:41.400
But I will say,
I feel like we put a lot more

00:01:41.400 --> 00:01:42.969
into our intervention for you.

00:01:42.969 --> 00:01:44.837
[ Laughter ]

00:01:44.837 --> 00:01:49.075
-Oh, what, because
I don't have a car waiting?

00:01:49.075 --> 00:01:50.376
-That would have been
really nice,

00:01:50.376 --> 00:01:52.545
if you had a car waiting
to take me to a tailor.

00:01:52.545 --> 00:01:54.780
-There's an SUV
to take you to Pennsylvania.

00:01:54.780 --> 00:01:57.383
You're going to spend two months
getting fitted.

00:01:57.383 --> 00:01:59.419
Listen, you're a 32 short,
right?

00:01:59.419 --> 00:02:01.187
[ Laughter ]

00:02:01.187 --> 00:02:03.590
-Did you really think I was
going to put pants on as well?

00:02:03.590 --> 00:02:05.057
Here, I'll put the shirt on.

00:02:05.057 --> 00:02:07.393
And while we're doing this --
-And look, I miced the jacket.

00:02:07.393 --> 00:02:09.662
-You did that?
-I did. Yeah, I did that.

00:02:09.662 --> 00:02:11.230
-You did that.
-No one that works here

00:02:11.230 --> 00:02:12.698
helped me.
-You know, while I'm doing this,

00:02:12.698 --> 00:02:14.300
because I do take this
seriously,

00:02:14.300 --> 00:02:17.636
you and Andy Samberg,
you co-hosted a talk show.

00:02:17.636 --> 00:02:19.172
You filled in for Jimmy Kimmel,

00:02:19.172 --> 00:02:21.941
and you guys took it super
seriously, so I get that...

00:02:21.941 --> 00:02:24.210
[ Laughter ]

00:02:24.210 --> 00:02:25.911
-But that was --

00:02:25.911 --> 00:02:29.148
we were satirizing
how badly you could dress.

00:02:29.148 --> 00:02:31.083
This is just --

00:02:31.083 --> 00:02:32.618
-You can sit down
while I change.

00:02:32.618 --> 00:02:33.852
You're making me very nervous.

00:02:33.852 --> 00:02:35.254
-What if you need --
-You feel like I'm gonna

00:02:35.254 --> 00:02:36.489
make a --
like I'm going to run?

00:02:36.489 --> 00:02:38.458
I'm going to run for it.
-No, it's like --

00:02:38.458 --> 00:02:40.126
My dad taught me,
at a restaurant,

00:02:40.126 --> 00:02:41.727
when your mother leaves
the table, you stand up.

00:02:41.727 --> 00:02:43.095
You know?
-Yeah, yeah.

00:02:43.095 --> 00:02:44.497
-Look at that.

00:02:44.497 --> 00:02:45.865
Look at it,
right over the crewneck.

00:02:45.865 --> 00:02:47.232
[ Laughter ]

00:02:47.232 --> 00:02:48.400
You're going to be miced twice.

00:02:48.400 --> 00:02:49.702
See, that's rare
in "Late Night."

00:02:49.702 --> 00:02:51.637
That'll go viral.
-This is --

00:02:51.637 --> 00:02:54.741
Yeah, for the kids
who love sound.

00:02:54.741 --> 00:02:56.976
[ Laughter ]

00:02:56.976 --> 00:02:58.678
Alright.
-Now, this is gonna be good.

00:02:58.678 --> 00:03:00.380
That way,
when I watch the show tonight,

00:03:00.380 --> 00:03:03.216
I won't think I'm watching
rehearsal, which I normally do.

00:03:03.216 --> 00:03:04.817
[ Laughter ]

00:03:04.817 --> 00:03:06.986
Do you feel a lot happier?
-Put on the tie!

00:03:06.986 --> 00:03:08.287
-[ Begrudgingly ] Alright.

00:03:08.287 --> 00:03:09.421
-You're on TV!

00:03:09.421 --> 00:03:11.357
-I feel like
if this would have had --

00:03:11.357 --> 00:03:13.593
this would have looked nice
if I did it backstage.

00:03:13.593 --> 00:03:15.795
-Well, there's no time for that.

00:03:15.795 --> 00:03:18.231
-How is your --
-Oh, good collar up?

00:03:18.231 --> 00:03:19.599
Yeah.

00:03:19.599 --> 00:03:20.800
[ Laughter ]

00:03:20.800 --> 00:03:23.303
See, people go,
"Now this is a show."

00:03:23.303 --> 00:03:25.571
[ Laughter, applause ]
This is real.

00:03:25.571 --> 00:03:26.739
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:03:26.739 --> 00:03:28.708
Look at that.

00:03:30.376 --> 00:03:32.511
-How was your first
Father's Day, buddy?

00:03:32.511 --> 00:03:33.946
Congratulations.
-Thank you very much.

00:03:33.946 --> 00:03:35.181
My first Father's Day was a...

00:03:35.181 --> 00:03:37.483
[ Cheers and applause ]
...beautiful affair.

00:03:37.483 --> 00:03:40.619
-What did you do?
-My son Malcolm,

00:03:40.619 --> 00:03:43.589
who's nearly seven months old,

00:03:43.589 --> 00:03:47.593
he got me a plethora of gifts.
-Oh, yeah.

00:03:47.593 --> 00:03:51.297
-I woke up,
and I had breakfast with him.

00:03:51.297 --> 00:03:54.633
And his new thing is,
when I turn the phone on

00:03:54.633 --> 00:03:57.570
and it's in selfie mode
and I put it on video,

00:03:57.570 --> 00:04:00.806
he grabs it and slowly brings
the camera into his mouth.

00:04:00.806 --> 00:04:04.711
So all his films
have the same twist ending.

00:04:04.711 --> 00:04:06.445
I think I have a photo
of him and me

00:04:06.445 --> 00:04:07.747
when we were on "SNL,"
I believe.

00:04:07.747 --> 00:04:09.148
-Oh, yeah, this is you.

00:04:09.148 --> 00:04:10.583
I mean, again,
it seems like your forcing --

00:04:10.583 --> 00:04:12.251
I'm not the only guy
you're forcing into a suit.

00:04:12.251 --> 00:04:13.953
-Yeah, see?

00:04:13.953 --> 00:04:16.789
I remember Malcolm's words were,
"If I'm going to 30 Rock

00:04:16.789 --> 00:04:18.457
or something
show business related,

00:04:18.457 --> 00:04:20.159
I should wear a goddamn suit."

00:04:20.159 --> 00:04:21.694
[ Laughter ]
-Well, sure,

00:04:21.694 --> 00:04:24.264
because he doesn't have
the job yet.

00:04:24.264 --> 00:04:25.965
I have the job.
-Yeah, you're right.

00:04:25.965 --> 00:04:27.266
You're right.

00:04:27.266 --> 00:04:29.368
-If I didn't have this job,
I'd be wearing a suit.

00:04:29.368 --> 00:04:31.270
-I know. The difference
between you and Malcolm.

00:04:31.270 --> 00:04:33.306
"Stature," he says every week.

00:04:33.306 --> 00:04:37.376
-I do.
Olivia posted this.

00:04:37.376 --> 00:04:40.212
It just said,
"My Valentines are watching --"

00:04:40.212 --> 00:04:41.014
Well, why don't you
tell everybody

00:04:41.014 --> 00:04:42.314
what you guys were watching?

00:04:42.314 --> 00:04:43.315
Because I think there are a lot
of different shows

00:04:43.315 --> 00:04:44.416
that kids like.

00:04:44.416 --> 00:04:45.885
And sometimes the adult,

00:04:45.885 --> 00:04:49.088
as a dad, you have to watch a
show that's maybe not for you.

00:04:49.088 --> 00:04:50.957
-Yeah, there's
that Cocomelon stuff.

00:04:50.957 --> 00:04:53.292
There's, you know,
the Daniel Tiger,

00:04:53.292 --> 00:04:55.094
and you watch these kid shows
when you have a baby.

00:04:55.094 --> 00:04:58.064
So here we are watching "Bosch"
on Amazon Prime,

00:04:58.064 --> 00:05:00.466
which is about Detective Bosch

00:05:00.466 --> 00:05:02.734
and based on
the Michael Connelly novels

00:05:02.734 --> 00:05:04.537
that kids love, right?

00:05:04.537 --> 00:05:06.071
And then recently,

00:05:06.071 --> 00:05:08.240
he and I were watching
"The Lincoln Lawyer" on Netflix.

00:05:08.240 --> 00:05:10.443
-Also Michael Connelly, right?
-And he goes, "Wait a second,

00:05:10.443 --> 00:05:11.377
are there some crossovers?"

00:05:11.377 --> 00:05:13.346
I go, "Malcolm, of course.

00:05:13.346 --> 00:05:16.415
The Lincoln lawyer
is Bosch's cousin."

00:05:16.415 --> 00:05:18.517
[ Laughter ]

00:05:18.517 --> 00:05:20.586
I mean, you should have seen
the look on his face.

00:05:20.586 --> 00:05:21.620
-Oh, yeah.

00:05:21.620 --> 00:05:24.056
When kids catch, like,
crossover stuff,

00:05:24.056 --> 00:05:26.158
they just love it.
-Yeah.

00:05:26.158 --> 00:05:27.360
My oldest boy.

00:05:27.360 --> 00:05:29.194
[ Laughter ]

00:05:29.194 --> 00:05:31.096
-You guys -- oh, wait.

00:05:31.096 --> 00:05:33.566
I'm wondering, because,
you know, we've got one as well.

00:05:33.566 --> 00:05:34.967
You know,
we've got a little baby.

00:05:34.967 --> 00:05:37.136
And I'm wondering if you have
felt the formula shortage,

00:05:37.136 --> 00:05:38.571
that has been something
that is --

00:05:38.571 --> 00:05:40.005
-Oh, yeah.
Baby formula shortage?

00:05:40.005 --> 00:05:44.477
Yes, yes. So, Malcolm drinks
Enfamil Gentlease.

00:05:44.477 --> 00:05:46.845
It's the purple label Enfamil.

00:05:46.845 --> 00:05:49.481
If anyone here has any,
I'll meet you in the parking lot

00:05:49.481 --> 00:05:52.919
and do -- I'll do all manner
of things to get that.

00:05:52.919 --> 00:05:54.386
[ Laughter ]

00:05:54.386 --> 00:05:58.223
So Olivia went to Target
and she goes,

00:05:58.223 --> 00:06:00.793
"You can only get four cans
at a time."

00:06:00.793 --> 00:06:02.528
-Okay.
-It can go pretty quickly.

00:06:02.528 --> 00:06:04.029
And so when she told me that,

00:06:04.029 --> 00:06:06.332
all my old drug-buying instincts
kicked in.

00:06:06.332 --> 00:06:09.268
I was like, "Oh, you're saying
we need to trick a pharmacy?

00:06:09.268 --> 00:06:10.669
Okay."

00:06:10.669 --> 00:06:12.905
I was like, "Let's get
some fake driver's licenses.

00:06:12.905 --> 00:06:15.374
I go in, I'm me. Four cans.
I come back a half hour later.

00:06:15.374 --> 00:06:17.043
Mr. Mustache, four more cans.

00:06:17.043 --> 00:06:19.078
You go in, you're you,
four cans.

00:06:19.078 --> 00:06:20.379
You come back,
old Vietnamese lady.

00:06:20.379 --> 00:06:21.514
Four more cans."

00:06:21.514 --> 00:06:22.748
[ Laughter ]

00:06:22.748 --> 00:06:24.917
Also I ordered some Enfamil
on Amazon.

00:06:24.917 --> 00:06:27.219
Have you ever gotten an Amazon
product and, like, it's janky?

00:06:27.219 --> 00:06:28.587
It's not the real thing?
-Yeah, yeah.

00:06:28.587 --> 00:06:30.489
-Terrible business.
Terrible business, Amazon.

00:06:30.489 --> 00:06:35.394
So I get a Enfamil
and I was like, "Is this real?

00:06:35.394 --> 00:06:36.461
I can't give this to my son."

00:06:36.461 --> 00:06:38.731
So I actually cut the bag open,

00:06:38.731 --> 00:06:40.933
dipped my finger in it went,

00:06:40.933 --> 00:06:44.403
"Yeah, that's really Enfamil."
[ Laughter ]

00:06:44.403 --> 00:06:46.239
-"This deal's good."
-"This deal's good."

00:06:46.239 --> 00:06:47.606
[ Laughter ]

00:06:47.606 --> 00:06:49.041
-I have a million more questions
for you.

00:06:49.041 --> 00:06:52.412
We will be right back
with more from John Mulaney!

